::ALG2_U05_UA_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=x+1 and y=5.
{
=A. x\=4#Correct. 5=x+1 gives x=4.
~B. x\=5#This is the y-value.
~C. x\=1#This is the intercept.
~D. x\=6#This adds instead of subtracting.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
Which point satisfies y>2x and y<6?
{
~A. (3,7)#7 is not less than 6.
~B. (2,3)#3 is not greater than 4.
~C. (4,9)#9 is not less than 6.
=D. (1,4)#Correct. 4>2 and 4<6.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
A nonlinear system has intersections at (-2,4) and (3,9). What are the solutions?
{
~A. x\=1 only#This is not listed as an intersection.
~B. No solution#Two intersections are given.
=C. x\=-2 and x\=3#Correct. System solutions are the intersection x-values with their points.
~D. x\=4 and x\=9#These are y-values only.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6
Question:
How does y=(x+2)² transform y=x²?
{
~A. Down 2#No outside -2 appears.
=B. Left 2#Correct. x+2 shifts left 2.
~C. Right 2#The inside sign is opposite.
~D. Up 2#No outside +2 appears.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=3x, what is f(g(x))?
{
=A. 3x+2#Correct. Substitute 3x into f.
~B. 3x²+2#This multiplies incorrectly.
~C. 4x+2#This adds functions.
~D. 3x-2#The constant sign is wrong.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=x+8.
{
~A. f⁻¹(x)\=x+8#This repeats the original.
~B. f⁻¹(x)\=8x#This does not undo addition.
~C. f⁻¹(x)\=1/(x+8)#Inverse notation is not reciprocal.
=D. f⁻¹(x)\=x-8#Correct. Subtract 8 to undo adding 8.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
What does solving f(x)=g(x) find?
{
~A. Only domain restrictions#Restrictions are allowed inputs.
~B. Only maximum values#Maxima are different features.
=C. Intersections#Correct. Equal outputs at the same input are intersections.
~D. Only y-intercepts#That checks x=0 only.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=2x and y=10.
{
~A. x\=20#This multiplies 10 by 2 instead of solving 10=2x by division.
=B. x\=5#Correct. 10=2x gives x=5.
~C. x\=10#This is the y-value from the horizontal equation, not the x-solution.
~D. x\=2#This is the coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
Which region satisfies y<=x+1?
{
=A. On or below the line#Correct. <= includes the boundary and values below.
~B. Above the line only#That would be >.
~C. Only the y-axis#The solution is a half-plane.
~D. No points#There are many solutions.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
If a system solution is (-1,3), what does it mean?
{
~A. Only the first equation is true#A system solution satisfies both.
~B. Only y\=0#The y-value is 3.
~C. The graphs never meet#A solution is a meeting point.
=D. Both equations are true at (-1,3)#Correct. The point satisfies both equations.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6
Question:
How does y=x²+5 transform y=x²?
{
~A. Left 5#Horizontal shifts are inside the input.
~B. Right 5#Horizontal shifts are inside the input.
=C. Up 5#Correct. Outside +5 shifts the graph up.
~D. Down 5#The sign is positive.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x-1, what is g(f(4))?
{
~A. 10#This adds instead of subtracting.
=B. 7#Correct. f(4)=8 and g(8)=7.
~C. 8#This stops after f(4).
~D. 6#This applies g to 4 first then doubles.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)=3x.
{
=A. f⁻¹(x)\=x/3#Correct. Divide by 3 to undo multiplying by 3.
~B. f⁻¹(x)\=3x#This repeats the original.
~C. f⁻¹(x)\=x-3#This undoes addition.
~D. f⁻¹(x)\=1/(3x)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
What is the average rate of change from (1,4) to (5,12)?
{
~A. 8#This is the output change.
~B. 4#This is the input change.
~C. 16#This adds coordinates.
=D. 2#Correct. (12-4)/(5-1)=2.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=x-3 and y=0.
{
~A. x\=0#This uses the given y-value as if it were the x-solution.
~B. x\=1#This does not satisfy the equation.
=C. x\=3#Correct. x-3=0.
~D. x\=-3#This reverses the sign; x-3=0 means x must equal 3.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
Which point satisfies y>=x² for x=2?
{
~A. (2,-1)#-1 is less than 4.
=B. (2,5)#Correct. 5>=4.
~C. (2,3)#3 is less than 4.
~D. (2,0)#0 is less than 4.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
A feasible solution must do what?
{
=A. Satisfy all constraints#Correct. Feasible means all restrictions are met.
~B. Satisfy one constraint only#All constraints matter.
~C. Ignore inequalities#Inequalities define feasibility.
~D. Be negative#Sign depends on context.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6
Question:
How does y=2x² compare to y=x²?
{
~A. Shift up 2#No outside addition appears.
~B. Shift right 2#No input change appears.
~C. Reflection#The coefficient is positive.
=D. Vertical stretch by 2#Correct. Outputs are doubled.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If (f+g)(x) for f=x and g=5x, what is the result?
{
~A. 4x#This subtracts.
~B. x+5#This treats g as constant.
=C. 6x#Correct. Add x+5x.
~D. 5x²#This multiplies.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
If f(x)=x/4, what is f⁻¹(x)?
{
~A. 1/(x/4)#This is reciprocal.
=B. 4x#Correct. Multiply by 4 to undo division.
~C. x/4#This repeats the original.
~D. x-4#This undoes addition.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q21::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Which graph feature occurs where y=0?
{
=A. x-intercept#Correct. x-intercepts have output 0.
~B. y-intercept#That occurs where x=0.
~C. domain#Domain is input set.
~D. range#Range is output set.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q22::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve 2x+1=9.
{
~A. x\=5#This adds instead of subtracting first.
~B. x\=8#This stops before dividing.
~C. x\=10#This adds values instead of isolating x after subtracting 1.
=D. x\=4#Correct. 2x=8, so x=4.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q23::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Which statement about f⁻¹(x) is correct?
{
~A. It always equals -f(x)#That is opposite, not inverse.
~B. It always has no graph#Inverse functions can be graphed.
=C. It reverses the input-output relationship#Correct. That is inverse function meaning.
~D. It always equals 1/f(x)#That is reciprocal confusion.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q24::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If f(2)=6 and g(2)=9, which is greater at x=2?
{
~A. Neither exists#Both values are given.
=B. g#Correct. 9 is greater than 6.
~C. f#6 is less than 9.
~D. They are equal#The outputs differ.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q25::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
For (f/g)(x), what must be excluded?
{
=A. Inputs where g(x)\=0#Correct. The denominator cannot be zero.
~B. Inputs where f(x)\=0#A zero numerator is allowed.
~C. All positive inputs#Positive inputs are not automatically excluded.
~D. All inputs#Only denominator zeros are excluded.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q26::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Solve x²=16.
{
~A. x\=4 only#This misses the negative root.
~B. x\=8#This is not a square root.
~C. x\=16#This is not a solution.
=D. x\=-4 and x\=4#Correct. Both square to 16.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q27::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6
Question:
Which transformation is a reflection over the x-axis?
{
~A. y\=f(x)+1#That shifts up.
~B. y\=f(x-1)#That shifts right.
=C. y\=-f(x)#Correct. Negating outputs reflects over the x-axis.
~D. y\=f(-x)#That reflects over the y-axis.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q28::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If f(x)=x+4 and g(x)=x-4, what is f(g(x))?
{
~A. 2x#This adds functions.
=B. x#Correct. The operations undo each other.
~C. x+8#This adds both shifts.
~D. x-8#This subtracts both shifts.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q29::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x)=2x-6?
{
=A. (x+6)/2#Correct. Add 6, then divide by 2.
~B. (x-6)/2#The sign is wrong.
~C. 2x+6#This does not reverse operations.
~D. 1/(2x-6)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q30::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Which value is not viable for number of items sold?
{
~A. 0#Selling zero items can be viable.
~B. 4#Positive counts can be viable.
~C. 12#Positive counts can be viable.
=D. -2#Correct. A count sold cannot be negative.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q31::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=3x and y=x+4.
{
~A. x\=6#This is y at the solution.
~B. x\=0#This does not satisfy both.
=C. x\=2#Correct. 3x=x+4 gives 2x=4.
~D. x\=4#This is not the x-solution.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q32::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
Which point satisfies y<5 and y>x?
{
~A. (5,5)#5 is not less than 5.
=B. (2,4)#Correct. 4<5 and 4>2.
~C. (6,4)#4 is not greater than 6.
~D. (2,6)#6 is not less than 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q33::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Which system type can have two intersections?
{
=A. A line and a parabola#Correct. A line and parabola can intersect twice.
~B. Two identical constants only#That description is incomplete.
~C. Only parallel lines#Parallel lines do not intersect.
~D. Only one horizontal line#One equation alone is not a system.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q34::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6
Question:
What does y=f(x)+3 do?
{
~A. Shift down 3#The sign is positive.
~B. Shift left 3#Horizontal changes are inside input.
~C. Reflect#No negative factor appears.
=D. Shift up 3#Correct. Adding outside shifts outputs up.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q35::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If f(x)=x² and g(x)=x+1, what is f(g(x))?
{
~A. x²(x+1)#This multiplies.
~B. 2x+1#This is not the composition.
=C. (x+1)²#Correct. Substitute x+1 into f.
~D. x²+1#This adds after squaring.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q36::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Which check verifies inverse functions?
{
~A. f(x)\=g(x)#Inverses are not usually identical.
=B. f(g(x))\=x and g(f(x))\=x#Correct. Both compositions must return x.
~C. f(x)+g(x)\=x#Addition is not the inverse check.
~D. f(x)g(x)\=1#This is reciprocal reasoning.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q37::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
A table shows outputs increasing by 5 whenever inputs increase by 1. What is the rate?
{
=A. 5#Correct. Output change per one input is 5.
~B. 1#This is the input change.
~C. 6#This adds changes.
~D. 0#The output is changing.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q38::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
What does a solution to a system of inequalities represent?
{
~A. Only an intersection point#Inequality systems often have regions.
~B. Only a y-intercept#That is one graph feature.
~C. A point outside every shaded region#That would not satisfy constraints.
=D. A point in the overlap of all solution regions#Correct. It must satisfy every inequality.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q39::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
Which statement best explains composition order in f(g(x))?
{
~A. Add f and g#Composition is not addition.
~B. Multiply f and g#Composition is not multiplication.
=C. Apply g first, then f#Correct. The inner function is evaluated first.
~D. Apply f first, then g#That would be g(f(x)).
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q40::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_UA_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
Why check restrictions after solving?
{
~A. It makes every answer negative#Restrictions depend on the situation.
=B. Some algebraic solutions may not be allowed#Correct. Restrictions and context can reject candidates.
~C. Restrictions never matter#They often matter.
~D. It changes correct equations into inequalities#Checking does not change problem type.
}

